---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { profile } from '../data/profile';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { profilePageSchema } from '../lib/jsonld';

const title = `About — ${profile.name}`;
const description = `${profile.role} at ${profile.employer}. ${profile.tagline}`;
---

<Base {title} {description} cwd="~" jsonLd={profilePageSchema()}>
  <span slot="command">cat about.md</span>

  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>
    I am a software engineer at Trilogy, where I am the product expert and
    engineering owner for <strong>six enterprise SaaS products</strong>: a
    customer advocacy platform, an employee social advocacy platform, a PaaS
    hosting provider, two CRM platforms and a customer support suite. Most of
    them arrived through acquisition, which means most of the code predates
    everyone currently working on it.
  </p>

  <p>
    That shapes the work. I spend my time in code I did not write, on products
    whose original authors are long gone, and I own them from the first
    reproduction of a customer defect through to the merged fix. Across two years
    that is <strong>580 defects closed</strong>, <strong>266 pull requests
    merged</strong>, and <strong>173 reviewed</strong> for other engineers, spread
    over <strong>56 repositories</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the agents come in</h2>

  <p>
    The more interesting half is what happened when I stopped doing that work by
    hand. AI coding agents were the obvious lever and, out of the box, useless
    here: a two-decade-old desktop CRM builds on Windows, and the iPad client
    needs real hardware to install and debug on. An agent that cannot reach the
    platform cannot do the work.
  </p>

  <p>
    So I built what it takes for them to work here. Harnesses that provision and
    drive <strong>Windows virtual machines</strong> and
    instrument <strong>physical iPads</strong>. A central knowledge base wired into the
    agents so they answer from real product knowledge rather than guessing, which
    cut onboarding onto an unfamiliar product by <strong>roughly 70
    percent</strong>. Then serverless pipelines that take a defect ticket through
    reproduction, crash dump analysis, fix, pull request, tests and review.
  </p>

  <p>
    The part I care most about is the check on all of it. An agent will happily
    write a regression test that passes against the broken code, which proves
    nothing at all. Every test has to be <strong>observed failing</strong> before
    the fix goes back in, enforced by a validation agent that rejects any test
    never seen to fail. Without that, the throughput is worth nothing.
  </p>

  <p>
    My own merged output went from <strong>41 changes in 2024 to 179 so far this
    year</strong>. The pipelines are used across the team now.
  </p>

  <h2>How I work</h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li><strong>Reproduce first.</strong> A fix for a bug you have not seen is a guess.</li>
    <li><strong>Fix it once, where every caller routes through.</strong> Patching the one path a ticket names leaves every sibling caller broken.</li>
    <li><strong>Watch the test fail before you trust it.</strong> A test that
      has never failed proves nothing, and that holds whether a person or an
      agent wrote it.</li>
    <li><strong>Small diffs.</strong> Reformatting unrelated code makes review harder for no gain.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
  <ul class="icon-links icon-links--marked">
    <li><a href={profile.links.github} rel="me noopener"><Icon name="github" />github.com/TanvirSingh007</a></li>
    <li><a href={profile.links.linkedin} rel="me noopener"><Icon name="linkedin" />linkedin</a></li>
    <li><a href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}><Icon name="email" />{profile.email}</a></li>
  </ul>

  <p class="note">
    Roles, dates and education are on the <a href="/experience/">experience
    page</a>.
  </p>

  <style>
    p strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }

    .plain { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 1.5rem; padding: 0; max-width: var(--measure); }
    .plain li { padding-left: 1.4rem; position: relative; margin-bottom: 0.6rem; color: var(--text-dim); }
    .plain li::before {
      content: '▸';
      position: absolute;
      left: 0;
      color: var(--accent);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.85em;
    }
    .plain strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }

    h2 { margin-top: 2.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.9rem; }
    .note { color: var(--text-dim); }
  </style>
</Base>
